$DIR/main.p:13:2: cannot store &x in the backing array of dst, which outlives x
    &x points into x. x is a local variable of g (declared at main.p:11). When g
    returns, x's memory is reclaimed, but the backing array of dst is reached
    through dst, which points to memory that g's caller owns and that lives
    longer. It would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store values instead of views of x, or allocate x and store its owner, an
    owned[int], which moves there:
        the backing array of dst = new(int)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
